# Which reader reads the files of @term/mill (call/code/role-of.ts reads this file: a manifest `role` line, else
# base/role.tree, else role.tree here).
#
# Every `.tree` under code/tree/ DEFINES a tree dialect: `code/tree/<dialect>/base.tree` names it (`mill fork` /
# `bind mine, load ./mine`), and its `mine.tree` and `mint.tree` hold the rules the toolchain reads with
# readMineGrammar and readMintGrammar (deck/make/code/compile/mill-load.ts). None of them is a program. Without this
# file every one was milled as Term code, so each `mine` rule read as a call to an undefined task and the editor
# showed over a thousand errors on one open grammar file.
#
# code/text/ and code/blob/ hold the format grammars (what @term/feed held until 2026-10-06), and those are NOT this
# role: `term make` builds each `mine.tree` there into a reader (call/code/make.ts), so they are left to the code
# role. note/term/plan/mill-merge-and-role-scopes.md.
#
# The `mill` role is read by deck/make/code/compile/mill-check.ts: the file parses, every `load` resolves, every
# `find` names something its target declares, and every `mint <x>, like <form>` names a form a load brings in.
# test/compile/mill-grammar.ts holds all 344 files to the same function.

role mill
  take @/code/tree/**/*.tree

# the `note` grammar's reader is Term the compiler runs (deck/make/code/compile/scope.tree hands it a grammar scope's
# text), written lean like every other module of the toolchain. Its mine.tree is the grammar itself, run by the text
# grammar engine, and is no code
role code
  mark lean
  take @/code/text/note/**/*.tree
    miss @/code/text/note/mine.tree
  take @/test/note/**/*.tree

# the LaTeX reader (note/term/mill/04-latex-grammar.md) is Term the same way, lean, and its mine.tree the grammar the
# reader is held to, run by the text grammar engine
role code
  mark lean
  take @/code/text/latex/**/*.tree
    miss @/code/text/latex/mine.tree
  take @/test/latex/**/*.tree
